| [[Wikipedia:Early 1980s recession|Early 1980s recession]]
| style="text-align:center;" |<span style="display:none">1981</span>July 1981&nbsp;–<br />Nov 1982
| style="text-align:center;" |<span style="display:none">16</span>1 year<br />4 months
| style="text-align:center;" |<span style="display:none">012</span>1 year
| style="text-align:center;" |<span style="display:none">10.8</span> 10.8%<br />(Nov 1982)
| style="text-align:center;" |<span style="display:none">02.7</span>−2.7%
| The [[Iranian Revolution]] sharply increased the price of oil around the world in 1979, causing the [[1979 energy crisis]]. This was caused by the new regime in power in [[Iran]], which exported oil at inconsistent intervals and at a lower volume, forcing prices up. Tight monetary policy in the United States to control inflation led to another recession. The changes were made largely because of inflation carried over from the previous decade because of the [[1973 oil crisis]] and the 1979 energy crisis.
|-
| [[Wikipedia:Early 1990s recession in the United States|Early 1990s recession in the United States]]
| style="text-align:center;" |<span style="display:none">1990</span>July 1990&nbsp;–<br />Mar 1991
| style="text-align:center;" |<span style="display:none">08</span>8 months
| style="text-align:center;" |<span style="display:none">092</span>7 years<br />8 months
| style="text-align:center;" |<span style="display:none">07.8</span> 7.8%<br />(June 1992)
| style="text-align:center;" |<span style="display:none">01.4</span>−1.4%
| After the lengthy peacetime expansion of the 1980s, inflation began to increase and the Federal Reserve responded by raising interest rates from 1986 to 1989. This weakened but did not stop growth, but some combination of the subsequent [[1990 oil price shock]], the debt accumulation of the 1980s, and growing consumer pessimism combined with the weakened economy to produce a brief recession.
|-
| [[Wikipedia:Early 2000s recession|Early 2000s recession]]
| style="text-align:center;" |<span style="display:none">2001</span>March 2001–Nov 2001
| style="text-align:center;" |<span style="display:none">08</span>8 months
| style="text-align:center;" |<span style="display:none">120</span>10 years
| style="text-align:center;" |<span style="display:none">06.3</span> 6.3%<br />(June 2003)
| style="text-align:center;" |<span style="display:none">00.3</span>−0.3%
| The 1990s were the longest period of growth in American history. The collapse of the speculative [[dot-com bubble]], a fall in business outlays and investments, and the [[Wikipedia:economic effects arising from the September 11 attacks|September 11th attacks]], brought the decade of growth to an end. Despite these major shocks, the recession was brief and shallow.
|-
| [[Wikipedia:Great Recession|Great Recession]]
| style="text-align:center;" |<span style="display:none">2007</span>Dec 2007 – June 2009
| style="text-align:center;" |<span style="display:none">18</span>1 year<br />6 months
| style="text-align:center;" |<span style="display:none">073</span>6 years<br />1 month
| style="text-align:center;" |<span style="display:none">09.7</span>10.0%<br />(October 2009)
| style="text-align:center;" |<span style="display:none">03.9</span>−5.1%
| The [[Wikipedia:subprime mortgage crisis|subprime mortgage crisis]] led to the collapse of the United States housing bubble. Falling housing-related assets contributed to a [[Wikipedia:Financial crisis of 2007–2010|global financial crisis]], even as oil and food prices soared. The crisis led to the failure or collapse of many of the United States' largest financial institutions: [[Wikipedia:Bear Stearns|Bear Stearns]], [[Wikipedia:Fannie Mae|Fannie Mae]], [[Wikipedia:Freddie Mac|Freddie Mac]], [[Wikipedia:Lehman Brothers|Lehman Brothers]], Citi Bank and [[Wikipedia:American International Group|AIG]], as well as a crisis in the [[Wikipedia:Automotive industry crisis of 2008–2009|automobile industry]]. The government responded with an unprecedented [[Wikipedia:Troubled Asset Relief Program|$700 billion bank bailout]] and [[Wikipedia:American Recovery and Reinvestment Act of 2009|$787 billion fiscal stimulus package]]. The National Bureau of Economic Research declared the end of this recession over a year after the end date. The Dow Jones Industrial Average finally reached its lowest point on March 9, 2009.
|-
| [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/COVID-19_recession Great Lockdown]
| style="text-align:center;" |<span style="display:none">2020</span>February
2020 – February 2021
| style="text-align:center;" |<span style="display:none">06</span>1 year
| style="text-align:center;" |<span style="display:none">05810</span>10 years<br />8 months
| style="text-align:center;" |<span style="display:none">07.8</span> 30.2%<br />(November 2020)
| style="text-align:center;" |<span style="display:none">02.2</span>−5.3%
| In response to the worldwide coronavirus pandemic, most of the United States instituted some form of social distancing that seriously reduced economic activity in the early 2020s and caused the highest spike in unemployment since the Great Depression.
|-
| 2025 Recession
| style="text-align:center;" |<span style="display:none">2018</span>March 2025 –November 2025
| style="text-align:center;" |<span style="display:none">06</span>8 months<br />
| style="text-align:center;" |<span style="display:none">0584</span> 4 years<br />1 month
| style="text-align:center;" |<span style="display:none">07.8</span> 4.3%<br />(May 2025)
| style="text-align:center;" |<span style="display:none">02.2</span>−0.5%
| After the expansion during the [[Little Cold War]], inflation began to increase and the Federal Reserve responded by raising interest rates from 2023 to 2025, while federal spending declined and undercut job growth. The calving of the South Greenland Ice Sheet compounded these issues with a market panic and a sharp drop in consumer confidence leading to a short recession with virtually no change in unemployment.
|-
| [[Market Crash of 2027|2027-29 recession]]
| style="text-align:center;" |<span style="display:none">2018</span>November 2027–December 2029
| style="text-align:center;" |<span style="display:none">06</span>2 years<br />1 month
| style="text-align:center;" |<span style="display:none">058</span>2 years
| style="text-align:center;" |<span style="display:none">07.8</span> -5%<br />(June 2029)
| style="text-align:center;" |<span style="display:none">02.2</span>−12.1%
| A continued decline in effective demand but a steady increase in the demand for non-effective demand led to a labor shortage and inflation. Coupled with the crash of the housing market and subsequent crash of the banking and financial services sector, the United States entered the worst recession in close to a century, marked by over-employment and rising living expenses rather than unemployment and falling prices.
|}

